@section 20.2 | Origin and Evolution of Life

@q 34:1-4

The early Earth's atmosphere had **methane, ammonia, hydrogen, water vapour and CO_{2}** but **no free oxygen** (Miller-Urey experiment, 1953, made amino acids from such a mix). **Cyanobacteria (blue-green algae)** were among the earliest organisms (fossil stromatolites over 3.5 billion years old) and their photosynthesis filled the air with oxygen.

@table
Theory | Scientist
Inheritance of acquired characters | **Lamarck** (1809)
**Natural selection** ("survival of the fittest" was Spencer's phrase) | **Charles Darwin** (*On the Origin of Species*, 1859), with A.R. Wallace
**Mutation theory** | **Hugo de Vries** (evening primrose)
Laws of inheritance | Gregor Mendel (1866)
@end

**Dinosaurs** were reptiles of the **Mesozoic era** (about 245-66 million years ago: Triassic, Jurassic, Cretaceous); they died out after an asteroid struck Chicxulub, Mexico. Birds evolved from small dinosaurs.

@section 20.3 | Classification and Microbes

@q 35:1

@table
Rank (higher to lower) | Example: human
Kingdom | Animalia
Phylum (division for plants) | Chordata
Class | Mammalia
Order | Primates
Family | Hominidae
Genus | *Homo*
Species | *sapiens*
@end

**Binomial nomenclature** (genus + species) was set by **Carl Linnaeus**, the father of taxonomy. **Whittaker** (1969) proposed **five kingdoms**: Monera, Protista, Fungi, Plantae, Animalia.

@q 35:2-4

Bacteria (0.5-5 µm) need a **compound microscope**; viruses need an **electron microscope**. Some bacteria are **autotrophic** (photosynthetic cyanobacteria, purple and green sulphur bacteria, chemosynthetic bacteria) but they have **no chloroplasts**; their pigments lie in membranes in the cytoplasm. **Viruses**, not bacteria, are called the link between living and non-living.

@section 20.4 | The Animal World

@q 35:5-9

@table
Animal | Group
**Seahorse** | True **fish**
Sea cucumber, starfish | Echinoderms
**Sea cow** (dugong), sea lion, whale, **dolphin**, **bat** | **Mammals**
**Sperm whale** | Largest **toothed** whale; **blue whale** (a baleen whale) is the largest animal ever
**Octopus**, squid, snail | **Molluscs**; the **colossal squid** is the largest invertebrate
**Spider**, scorpion, tick | **Arachnids** (8 legs), not insects
Echidna, platypus | **Egg-laying mammals** (monotremes)
Kangaroo, koala | Pouched mammals (marsupials)
@end

Whales and dolphins are mammals and breathe air through a **blowhole**; **earthworms** breathe through their moist **skin**. An earthworm has **several pairs of "hearts"** (usually four or five pairs of aortic arches), not two, and is the **farmer's friend** because it aerates and enriches soil.

